Title: Getting Started — Koka Tutorial
[INCLUDE=../sidebar-sub.mdk]

# Getting Started { .heading-no-number }

Let's write our first Koka program.

---

```koka {.hidden}
import std/num/float64
```

## Hello, World!

Create a file called `hello.kk` with this content:

```koka
fun main()
  println("Hello, world!")
```

Run it with:

```unchecked
koka hello.kk
```

You should see `Hello, world!` printed to the terminal.

A few things to notice right away:

- Functions are declared with `fun`
- The body is **indented** — no curly braces needed (though you can use them)
- `println` is a standard library function

If you use VS Code with the Koka extension, public functions named `main`, `example...`, or
`test...` also get run/debug CodeLens actions directly in the editor.

---

## Running Files and Samples

You can compile any installed sample directly from the command line. For example, the Caesar
cipher sample compiles as:

```unchecked
$ koka samples/basic/caesar.kk
compile: samples/basic/caesar.kk
loading: std/core
loading: std/core/types
...
check  : samples/basic/caesar
linking: samples_basic_caesar
created: .koka/.../samples_basic_caesar
```

Running the produced executable prints:

```unchecked
$ .koka/.../samples_basic_caesar
plain  : Koka is a well-typed language
encoded: Krnd lv d zhoo-wbshg odqjxdjh
cracked: Koka is a well-typed language
```

The exact build directory depends on your platform and backend, but the workflow is always the same:
compile with `koka ...`, then run the produced executable.

---

## Expressions and Values

Koka is built around _expressions_ — everything that produces a value.
Open the REPL (`koka`) and try a few:

```unchecked
> 1 + 2
3
> "hello" ++ " " ++ "world"
"hello world"
> True && False
False
```

Operators like `++` (string concatenation) and `&&` (boolean and) work as you'd expect.

The REPL also supports useful commands for inspecting and loading code:

```unchecked
> :t "hi"
string

> :t println("hi")
console ()

> :l samples/basic/fibonacci
> main()
...
```

Use `:t` to inspect the inferred type of an expression, and `:l` to load a file or sample module.

For performance-oriented experiments, the REPL can also show elapsed timings and reload quickly:

```unchecked
> :set --showtime
> :l samples/basic/rbtree.kk
> main()
...
420000
info: elapsed: ...

> :set -O2
> :r
> main()
...
420000
info: elapsed: ...
```

This is a convenient loop for comparing debug and optimized runs without leaving the REPL.

---

## Basic Types

Koka's most common built-in types:

| Type | Examples | Description |
|------|---------|-------------|
| `int` | `42`, `-7`, `0` | Arbitrary precision integer |
| `float64` | `3.14`, `-0.5` | 64-bit floating point |
| `string` | `"hello"` | Text string |
| `bool` | `True`, `False` | Boolean |
| `char` | `'a'`, `'\n'` | Unicode character |

The `show` function converts most values to a string:

```unchecked
import std/num/float64
```
```koka
fun example1()
  println(42.show)
  println((3.14).show)
  println(True.show)
```

---

## `val` — Naming Values

Use `val` to give a name to a value within a function:

```koka
fun example2()
  val greeting = "Hello"
  val name     = "world"
  println(greeting ++ ", " ++ name ++ "!")
```

`val` bindings are **immutable** — once set, the value never changes. This is
the default in Koka and most functional languages.

---

## `var` — Mutable Variables

When you truly need to update a value, use `var` with `:=` for assignment:

```koka
fun example3()
  var count := 0
  count := count + 1
  count := count + 1
  println(count.show)   // prints 2
```

`var` is the exception, not the rule — most code in Koka uses `val` or function
parameters and never mutates anything.

---

## Conditionals

Koka's `if` is an _expression_ — it produces a value:

```koka
fun describe(n : int) : string
  if n < 0 then "negative"
  elif n == 0 then "zero"
  else "positive"

fun example4()
  println(describe(-3))
  println(describe(0))
  println(describe(5))
```

Because `if` is an expression, both branches must have the same type.

---

## Type Annotations

Koka infers types automatically — you saw `describe` above already has type
annotations on its parameter and return type. These are optional but good practice:

```koka
fun add( x : int, y : int ) : int
  x + y
```

If you leave them off, Koka still infers the types — hover over identifiers in VS Code
to see what was inferred.

In practice it is still a good habit to annotate function parameters and return types:
it improves documentation, gives clearer compiler feedback, and can help disambiguate overloaded library functions.

---

## The `unit` Type

Some functions perform an action but don't return a meaningful value.
They return `()`, pronounced _unit_, which is Koka's equivalent of `void`:

```koka
fun greet( name : string ) : console ()
  println("Hello, " ++ name ++ "!")
```

The `: ()` return type annotation can also be omitted when returning unit.

---

## Working with the REPL

The REPL is useful for exploring types and testing expressions interactively.
Here are the most useful commands:

```unchecked
> :l samples/basic/caesar       // load a file
> :t encode                     // show the type of a function
> encode(3,"hello")             // evaluate an expression
> :r                            // reload after editing
> :set --showtime               // show execution time
> :set -O2                      // optimize
> :q                            // quit
```

Use `:t` frequently -- it shows the inferred effect type, which is how
you verify that Koka is tracking effects correctly.

---

## Summary

In this section you learned:
- How to write and run a Koka program
- How to compile and run installed sample programs from the command line
- How to use the REPL commands `:t`, `:l`, `:r`, and `:set`
- The basic built-in types: `int`, `float64`, `string`, `bool`, `char`
- `val` for immutable names, `var` for mutable variables
- `if`/`elif`/`else` as expressions
- Type annotations (and that inference makes them optional)

